// An unknown type is reported wherever a declaration names one — and that has to include a GENERIC head.
// It did not: `checkTypeResolves` bailed outright on `genericArg`, so
//
//     Nonexistent  a;          // reported: unknown type
//     Nonexistent<int32> a;    // ACCEPTED, before this rule
//
// The spelling carrying MORE type information got LESS checking, and the mistake surfaced later as a clang
// 'undeclared identifier' against generated code. It is also what let the old `Ptr<T>` spelling survive
// `kama check` after the rename to `UnsafePtr<T>`.
//
// The generic ARGUMENT is deliberately still not walked here: `InlineArray<int32>#(N)`'s `N` is a
// const-generic value, not a type, and the argument position has its own checks.
type value Holder {
    public Nonexistent<int32> a;
    public ctor make() { this.a = 0; }
}

fn int32 main() { return 0; }
